   "implant_note": "VBR replaces parts of the fractured vertebral body and the adjacent discs; posterior internal fixator remains in situ",
   "source": "Rohlmann 2014c gives only group ranges (4 M/1 F, 62-71 y, 60-74 kg, 168-180 cm, BMI 19-26, L1 in WP1-WP4, L3 in WP5); per-patient values taken from the same WP1-WP5 cohort tables (Rohlmann 2014a Table 1 / Dreischarf 2015 Table 1)",
   "disc_area_mm2": null
  },
  "notes": "Load measured on a telemeterised vertebral body replacement (modified Synex, 6 load components, accuracy ~2% force / 5% moment of 3000 N / 20 Nm calibration range) that shares the load with the posterior internal fixator, residual vertebral bone and bone graft; the VBR force is NOT the total spinal load. Evaluation: peak resultant force during the exercise minus the mean resultant force in the relaxed resting position (same posture) = force increase. Per patient: median and range over trials; \"Average\" bar = median of the patient medians with range of the medians. Lying resultant VBR force was usually <50 N (up to 100 N with muscle tension). Only general instructions were given; trials span ~5 years post-op. Text: one-leg raise average increase only 10 N (WP5 nearly 100 N); both legs nearly 220 N; pelvis lifting average ~250 N with large intra-individual variation depending on pelvis height. n totals match Table 1 (94, 41, 93)."
 },
